const NHAP = {"1-qa.csv":"No,Spec ref,Category,Question,Our proposal,Priority,Need by,Asked on,Answered by,Answer,Status\n9,TC-42,UAT,What time the reservation is released? Is 11:01 correct?,,High,,2026-11-30,,,Open\nQ-010,,UAT,Please explain about notification in TC-43,,Normal,,2026-11-30,,,Open\n","1-standup.txt":"Yesterday I fix the bug of search screen and I am test the no-show job, it is almost done, maybe 90% complete and I will try to finish it today because UAT is start today and Priya team will test it so I need to make sure everything is okay and there is no problem.\nToday I continue.\n","2-bug.txt":"Title: No-show bug\n\nEnvironment\n  UAT\n\nSteps to reproduce\n  1. I set the limit of Lab A to 60 minutes\n  2. I reserved an item and did not pick up\n\nExpected result\n  Not released\n\nActual result\n  The reservation was released at 10:30.\n\nSeverity / Priority\n  High\n","2-chat-priya.txt":"Hi Priya, sorry for the bug. Sorry, I will check it ASAP and revert back to you EOD.\n","3-commits.txt":"fixed bug\n---\nUpdate code for LAB-331.\n---\nfix: review\n","3-pr.md":"## Summary\nFix bug no-show.\n\n## How to test\nRun the app and check.\n\n## Risks\nNone\n","3-review-reply.txt":"OK anh, em sẽ sửa. About (2) I think it is no problem.\n","4-email-tre.txt":"Subject: Sorry\n\nDear Sir/Madam,\n\nSorry, because of the bug LAB-331 we were very busy yesterday. We tried our best but we could not finish the document. We are very sorry for this.\n\nPlease wait until tomorrow.\n\nBest regards,\nAn\n","4-minutes.md":"Meeting today\n\n- LAB-331 will deploy tomorrow\n- Notification is email\n- Priya will test again\n- Tanaka-san will answer Q-010 next week\n","5-bao-cao.txt":"Subject: Report\n\nHi Tanaka-san,\n\nThis week we had UAT. There was a bug LAB-331 and some issues with the server but now it is fixed. The document CR-004 is almost done.\n\nNext week we will continue UAT and fix bugs ASAP.\n\nThanks,\nAn\n","5-demo.txt":"Hello. This is the new version. I fix the bug. You can see the limit is 60 minutes now and the reservation is not released at 10:30 and it is released at 11:00 like the spec say and also the manager get email.\nIf you want Excel export also, of course we can do that.\n","5-su-co-1.txt":"Hi all, UAT server is DOWN!!! We are checking. Sorry.\n","5-su-co-2.txt":"Fixed. Sorry for the inconvenience, sorry.\n"};
const NOP = {"1-qa.csv":"No,Spec ref,Category,Question,Our proposal,Priority,Need by,Asked on,Answered by,Answer,Status\nQ-009,\"UAT TC-42, SRS 3.5.2\",No-show,\"With a 60-minute limit and a 10:00-11:00 slot, is the reservation released at 11:00 (start + 60 minutes)?\",\"Yes, 11:00. The job runs every minute, so the status changes between 11:00 and 11:01. We suggest changing TC-42 to \"\"at 11:01 the reservation is Released\"\".\",High,2026-12-02,2026-11-30,,,Open\nQ-010,UAT TC-43,No-show,\"When a reservation is released, should the student who made it also get the email, not only the lab manager?\",\"Yes, both get an email. Option B: only the lab manager, as TC-43 says now. Both options take less than one day.\",Medium,2026-12-07,2026-11-30,,,Open\n","1-standup.txt":"Yesterday I finished the tests for LAB-315, the no-show release job, and deployed build 6.2 to the UAT server.\nToday Priya's team starts UAT. I'm going to watch the results and fix any bug from UAT first.\nI also sent two questions about the test cases, Q-009 and Q-010. No blockers for now.\n","2-bug.txt":"Title: No-show job releases reservations after 30 minutes when the lab limit is 60 minutes\n\nEnvironment\n  UAT server, build 6.2 (API 2.0.0-rc.2), Chrome 142, user priya.qa (lab manager of Lab A)\n\nSteps to reproduce\n  1. Open Settings > Lab A and set \"No-show limit\" to 60 minutes.\n  2. As a student, reserve any item of Lab A for 10:00-11:00 today.\n  3. Do not pick up the item.\n  4. At 10:31, open My reservations.\n\nExpected result\n  The reservation is still Reserved until 11:00 (SRS 3.5.2, Q-007, UAT TC-41).\n\nActual result\n  The reservation is Released at 10:30. The history shows \"Released by system (no-show)\".\n\nFrequency\n  Always (3/3, reported by Priya Nair on 1 Dec 2026).\n\nSeverity / Priority\n  Major / P1. Blocks UAT TC-41 and TC-42.\n\nAttachments & notes\n  Screenshot from Priya (chat, 1 Dec 09:12 ICT). The default limit is 30 minutes,\n  so the job may be reading the default instead of the lab setting.\n","2-chat-priya.txt":"Thanks, Priya, for the clear steps. I can reproduce it: the reservation is released at 10:30 instead of 11:00. I filed it as LAB-331 (Major). I'll send you a fix time by 1 Dec 15:00 ICT (13:30 IST). Until then, please skip TC-41 and TC-42.\n","3-commits.txt":"fix(reservation): use the lab's no-show limit in the release job\n\nThe job read the default limit (30 minutes) for every lab, so a lab\nwith a 60-minute limit lost its reservations 30 minutes early.\nLoad each lab's limit and fall back to the default only when the\nlab has no value.\n\nCloses LAB-331\n---\nperf(reservation): load lab limits once per job run\n\nThe first fix ran one query per reservation. Read all labs in one\nquery before the loop. 2 queries per run instead of N+1.\n\nRefs LAB-331\n---\ntest(reservation): cover labs without a no-show limit\n\nOld labs created before 2.0 have no value. The job must use the\ndefault of 30 minutes for them.\n\nRefs LAB-331\n","3-pr.md":"## Summary\nThe no-show job used the default limit (30 minutes) for every lab. It now uses each lab's own limit (15-120 minutes, Q-007).\nCloses LAB-331.\n\n## Changes\n- `ReleaseJob` loads all labs once per run and reads `noShowLimitMinutes` from each lab.\n- Labs without a value (created before 2.0) use the default of 30 minutes.\n- 3 new tests: 60-minute lab, lab without a value, lab set to 15 minutes.\n\n## How to test\n1. Set the Lab A limit to 60 minutes.\n2. Reserve an item for a slot that starts in 2 minutes and do not pick it up.\n3. Run `npm run job:release -- --now=+35m` → the reservation should still be Reserved.\n4. Run `npm run job:release -- --now=+61m` → it should be Released.\n\n## Risks\n- Labs with a wrong value in old data (for example 0) will release immediately. I checked UAT data: 0 rows with a value outside 15-120.\n- Not included: the release email. Who receives it is still open (Q-010).\n","3-review-reply.txt":"Thanks, Minh. (1) Good catch: the job now loads all labs in one query before the loop, 2 queries per run instead of N+1 (commit perf(reservation)). (2) Old labs have no value, so the job uses the default of 30 minutes. I added a test for that case. Could you take another look?\n","4-email-tre.txt":"Subject: [LabFlow] CR-004 design document: new date Fri 4 Dec 17:00 JST\nTo: Tanaka Yui\nCc: Minh\n\nDear Tanaka-san,\n\nThe CR-004 (Excel export) design document will be ready by Fri 4 Dec 17:00 JST, one day later than I promised. I'm sorry for the change. No action is needed from you.\n\nThe reason: on 1 Dec we found bug LAB-331 in UAT, and we fixed it first so that Priya's team can continue testing.\n\nWhat is ready now:\n- the export columns and the file format (about 80% of the document)\nWhat is left:\n- the screen design and the estimate for the two options\n\nIf you need the column list earlier for your internal meeting, I can send that part today. Please let me know.\n\nBest regards,\nAn\n","4-minutes.md":"Subject: [LabFlow] Minutes: client call, 3 Dec 2026\n\nDate: Thu 3 Dec 2026, 10:00-10:30 ICT (12:00-12:30 JST, 08:30-09:00 IST)\nAttendees: Tanaka Yui, Priya Nair (client); Minh, An (FPT Software)\n\n## Decisions\n1. The LAB-331 fix goes into build 6.3, deployed to UAT on 4 Dec 2026 at 10:00 ICT.\n2. Q-009: 11:00 is correct. TC-42 will be changed to \"at 11:01 the reservation is Released\".\n3. TC-43: in phase 1 the release notification is sent by email only.\n\n## Action items\n- @An: deploy build 6.3 to the UAT server, by 4 Dec 2026 10:00 ICT\n- @Priya: re-run TC-41 to TC-44 on build 6.3, by 4 Dec 2026 17:00 IST\n- @Priya: update TC-42 in the test cases, by 4 Dec 2026\n- @Tanaka: answer Q-010, by 7 Dec 2026\n\n## Open questions\n- Q-010 (does the student also get the release email?): Tanaka-san will answer by 7 Dec 2026.\n\nIf anything here is not correct, please reply by 4 Dec 2026.\n","5-bao-cao.txt":"Subject: [LabFlow] Weekly report, 30 Nov - 4 Dec 2026\nTo: Tanaka Yui\nCc: Priya Nair, Minh\n\nDear Tanaka-san,\n\nHere is our weekly report. No action is needed from you, except Q-010 by 7 Dec.\n\nDone this week:\n- UAT started on 30 Nov. Priya's team ran 38 of 44 test cases.\n- LAB-331 (no-show limit) fixed in build 6.3. TC-41 to TC-44 passed on 4 Dec.\n- UAT server was down for 50 minutes on 4 Dec (10:05-10:55 ICT) after a migration. Report sent.\n\nNext week (7-11 Dec):\n- Fix the UAT bugs found this week, starting with the Major ones.\n- Send the CR-004 estimate with two options by 8 Dec.\n\nRisks:\n- If Q-010 (who gets the release email) is not answered by 7 Dec, the email may not be ready for build 6.4.\n\nBest regards,\nAn\n","5-demo.txt":"Good afternoon, Tanaka-san. Today I'll show you the fix for LAB-331, the no-show limit for each lab. It takes about three minutes.\nFirst, let me show you the settings of Lab A. As you can see, the no-show limit is now 60 minutes.\nNext, I reserve a microscope from 10:00 to 11:00 and I don't pick it up.\nNow it's 10:45 on the test clock. The reservation is still Reserved.\nThen at 11:01 the job runs, and the status changes to Released.\nFinally, the lab manager gets an email, as we agreed yesterday.\nTo sum up, each lab now uses its own limit, from 15 to 120 minutes. Next steps: Priya's team re-runs TC-41 to TC-44 today.\nDo you have any questions?\n","5-su-co-1.txt":"[Investigating] UAT server: every page returns 502 since 10:05 ICT (12:05 JST), right after we deployed build 6.3. Please pause UAT for now. We are looking at it and will post the next update by 10:40 ICT (12:40 JST).\n","5-su-co-2.txt":"[Resolved] UAT server is back since 10:55 ICT (12:55 JST). Cause: the new database migration in build 6.3 locked the reservation table. We rolled back to 6.2, fixed the migration and deployed 6.3 again. No UAT data was lost. You can continue UAT now. A short report will follow by 4 Dec 16:00 ICT.\n"};
const PV = {"KeyDemo.java":"import java.util.*;\n\npublic class KeyDemo {\n    // A key class WITHOUT equals() and hashCode(): Java compares object addresses.\n    static class TicketKey {\n        final String id;\n        TicketKey(String id) { this.id = id; }\n    }\n\n    // The same key as a record: equals() and hashCode() are generated from the fields.\n    record TicketRecord(String id) {}\n\n    // A mutable key: its hash changes after it is stored.\n    static class MutableKey {\n        String id;\n        MutableKey(String id) { this.id = id; }\n        @Override public boolean equals(Object o) { return o instanceof MutableKey k && k.id.equals(id); }\n        @Override public int hashCode() { return id.hashCode(); }\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Map<TicketKey, String> a = new HashMap<>();\n        a.put(new TicketKey(\"LAB-331\"), \"Major\");\n        System.out.println(\"1. no equals/hashCode: get -> \" + a.get(new TicketKey(\"LAB-331\")) + \", size \" + a.size());\n\n        Map<TicketRecord, String> b = new HashMap<>();\n        b.put(new TicketRecord(\"LAB-331\"), \"Major\");\n        System.out.println(\"2. record:             get -> \" + b.get(new TicketRecord(\"LAB-331\")) + \", size \" + b.size());\n\n        Map<MutableKey, String> c = new HashMap<>();\n        MutableKey k = new MutableKey(\"LAB-331\");\n        c.put(k, \"Major\");\n        k.id = \"LAB-332\";\n        System.out.println(\"3. key changed after put: get(LAB-331) -> \" + c.get(new MutableKey(\"LAB-331\"))\n                + \", get(LAB-332) -> \" + c.get(new MutableKey(\"LAB-332\")) + \", size \" + c.size());\n\n        System.out.println(\"   \\\"LAB-331\\\".hashCode() = \" + \"LAB-331\".hashCode() + \", \\\"LAB-332\\\".hashCode() = \" + \"LAB-332\".hashCode());\n    }\n}\n","deadlock.sh":"#!/bin/bash\n# Two sessions update the same two rows in opposite order -> PostgreSQL detects the deadlock.\nset -u\npsql -q -U postgres -c \"CREATE TABLE lab (id int PRIMARY KEY, no_show_limit int); INSERT INTO lab VALUES (1, 30), (2, 30);\"\n( psql -U postgres -v ON_ERROR_STOP=1 <<'S1'\nBEGIN;\nUPDATE lab SET no_show_limit = 60 WHERE id = 1;\nSELECT pg_sleep(1);\nUPDATE lab SET no_show_limit = 60 WHERE id = 2;\nCOMMIT;\nS1\n) > /tmp/s1.txt 2>&1 &\nP=$!\nsleep 0.3\npsql -U postgres <<'S2' > /tmp/s2.txt 2>&1\nBEGIN;\nUPDATE lab SET no_show_limit = 45 WHERE id = 2;\nSELECT pg_sleep(1);\nUPDATE lab SET no_show_limit = 45 WHERE id = 1;\nCOMMIT;\nS2\nwait $P\necho \"--- session 1\"; cat /tmp/s1.txt\necho \"--- session 2\"; cat /tmp/s2.txt\necho \"--- table after both sessions\"; psql -U postgres -c \"SELECT * FROM lab ORDER BY id;\"\n","index.sql":"CREATE TABLE reservation (id serial PRIMARY KEY, lab_id int, student_code text, status text, start_at timestamptz);\nINSERT INTO reservation (lab_id, student_code, status, start_at)\nSELECT g % 20, 'SE' || (100000 + g), (ARRAY['RESERVED','RELEASED','DONE'])[g % 3 + 1], '2026-01-01'::timestamptz + g * interval '7 minutes'\nFROM generate_series(1, 200000) g;\nANALYZE reservation;\n\\echo '--- 1. no index'\nEXPLAIN (ANALYZE, COSTS OFF, TIMING OFF, SUMMARY OFF) SELECT * FROM reservation WHERE student_code = 'SE150000';\nCREATE INDEX idx_reservation_student ON reservation (student_code);\nANALYZE reservation;\n\\echo '--- 2. with an index on student_code'\nEXPLAIN (ANALYZE, COSTS OFF, TIMING OFF, SUMMARY OFF) SELECT * FROM reservation WHERE student_code = 'SE150000';\n\\echo '--- 3. same index, but a function on the column'\nEXPLAIN (ANALYZE, COSTS OFF, TIMING OFF, SUMMARY OFF) SELECT * FROM reservation WHERE lower(student_code) = 'se150000';\n","pv-nghe-2.txt":"[   0.0s ->    3.5s] Object-oriented programming has four main ideas.\n[   3.5s ->    8.3s] Encapsulation, inheritance, polymorphism and abstraction.\n[   8.3s ->   13.8s] In my lab booking project, the reservation class keeps its status private.\n[  13.8s ->   17.8s] You can only change it through methods like approve or release.\n[  17.8s ->   19.7s] That's encapsulation.\n[  19.7s ->   22.9s] No other class can set an invalid status.\n[  22.9s ->   24.2s] Polymorphism.\n[  24.2s ->   27.4s] Our code calls send on a nodeifier interface,\n[  27.4s ->   30.6s] and the email version, or a test version runs.\n[  30.6s ->   35.6s] I use inheritance less, because a deep class tree is hard to change.\n[  35.6s ->   39.6s] A hash map stores key value pairs in an array of buckets.\n[  39.6s ->   42.8s] It calls hash code on the key to choose a bucket,\n[  42.8s ->   46.6s] and equals to find the right key inside that bucket.\n[  46.6s ->   50.5s] So if a class used as a key doesn't override both methods,\n[  50.5s ->   53.7s] get returns null for a key that looks the same.\n[  53.7s ->   54.9s] I tested it.\n[  54.9s ->   60.0s] With a plain class the lookup returned L, and with a Java record it worked.\n[  60.0s ->   63.0s] Lookups are constant time on average.\n[  63.0s ->   66.2s] And never change a key after you put it in the map.\n[  66.2s ->   70.6s] An index is a separate sorted structure, usually a B-tree,\n[  70.6s ->   74.7s] so the database can find rows without reading the whole table.\n[  74.7s ->   79.8s] I tested it in PostgreSQL with 200,000 reservations.\n[  79.8s ->   83.8s] Without an index, the query did a sequential scan.\n[  83.8s ->   88.2s] With an index on the student code, it used an index scan.\n[  88.2s ->   93.0s] But the index didn't help when I wrapped the column in the lower function.\n[  93.0s ->   99.8s] Indexes also make inserts slower, so I add them for real queries, not for every column.\n[  99.8s ->  104.8s] A deadlock is when two transactions each hold a lock that the other one needs,\n[ 104.8s ->  106.7s] so neither can continue.\n[ 106.7s ->  109.6s] I reproduced one in PostgreSQL.\n[ 109.6s ->  111.8s] Session 1 updated lab 1.\n[ 111.8s ->  113.3s] Then lab 2.\n[ 113.3s ->  124.0s] Session 2 updated lab 2, then lab 1. After about one second, PostgreSQL detected the deadlock and cancelled one transaction.\n[ 124.0s ->  132.5s] To avoid it, I update rows in the same order, keep transactions short, and retry the transaction that was cancelled.\n[ 132.5s ->  142.6s] 401, unauthorized, means the server doesn't know who you are. The token is missing or expired, so the client has to log in again.\n[ 142.6s ->  149.3s] 403, forbidden, means the server knows who you are, but you're not allowed to do this.\n[ 149.3s ->  155.8s] For example, in our system a student who calls the endpoint for approvals gets a 403,\n[ 155.8s ->  161.5s] because only lab managers can approve. The n plus one problem is when the code runs\n[ 161.5s ->  166.9s] one query to load a list, and then one more query for each item in the list.\n[ 166.9s ->  170.4s] With JPA it often happens with lazy relations.\n[ 170.4s ->  177.8s] We load 50 reservations, and then each reservation loads its lab, so that's 51 queries.\n[ 177.8s ->  183.8s] The fix is a join fetch, or an entity graph. I had a similar case in a code review.\n[ 183.8s ->  190.2s] My first fix ran one query per reservation, and my lead asked me to load the lab's ones.\n[ 190.2s ->  196.8s] I'd like to join FPT software, because I want to work on real projects for foreign clients,\n[ 196.8s ->  200.1s] and learn how a large team delivers software.\n[ 200.1s ->  203.0s] I've already practiced that way of working.\n[ 203.0s ->  207.5s] I write tickets, pull requests, and queue and lists in English,\n[ 207.5s ->  210.6s] and I record my stand-ups to check them.\n[ 210.6s ->  215.6s] During the internship, I want to become a reliable backend developer in Java,\n[ 215.6s ->  220.1s] and spring, and get better at working with Japanese clients.\n[ 220.1s ->  222.6s] Yes, I have two questions.\n[ 222.6s ->  226.6s] What does a typical first month look like for an intern on your team?\n[ 226.8s ->  233.8s] And how do you give feedback to interns, for example in code reviews or one-to-one meetings?\n--- 576 words in 233.8 s = 148 words per minute\n","pv-nghe.txt":"[   0.0s ->    3.5s] Object-oriented programming has four main ideas.\n[   3.5s ->    8.2s] Encapsulation, inheritance, polymorphism and abstraction.\n[   8.2s ->   13.8s] In my lab booking project, the reservation class keeps its status private.\n[  13.8s ->   17.8s] You can only change it through methods like approve or release.\n[  17.8s ->   19.7s] That's encapsulation.\n[  19.7s ->   22.9s] No other class can set an invalid status.\n[  22.9s ->   24.2s] Polymorphism.\n[  24.2s ->   27.4s] Our code calls send on a nodeifier interface,\n[  27.4s ->   30.6s] and the email version, or a test version runs.\n[  30.6s ->   35.6s] I use inheritance less, because a deep class tree is hard to change.\n[  35.6s ->   39.6s] A hash map stores key value pairs in a array of buckets.\n[  39.6s ->   42.8s] It calls hash code on the key to choose a bucket,\n[  42.8s ->   46.6s] and equals to find the right key inside that bucket.\n[  46.6s ->   50.5s] So if a class used as a key doesn't override both methods,\n[  50.5s ->   53.7s] get returns null for a key that looks the same.\n[  53.7s ->   54.9s] I tested it.\n[  54.9s ->   60.0s] With a plain class the lookup returned L, and with a Java record it worked.\n[  60.0s ->   66.2s] Lookups are constant time on average, and never change a key after you put it in the map.\n[  66.2s ->   70.6s] An index is a separate sorted structure, usually a B-tree,\n[  70.6s ->   74.7s] so the database can find rows without reading the whole table.\n[  74.7s ->   79.8s] I tested it in PostgreSQL with 200,000 reservations.\n[  79.8s ->   83.8s] Without an index, the query did a sequential scan.\n[  83.8s ->   88.2s] With an index on the student code, it used an index scan.\n[  88.2s ->   93.0s] But the index didn't help when I wrapped the column in the lower function.\n[  93.0s ->   99.8s] Indexes also make inserts slower, so I add them for real queries, not for every column.\n[  99.8s ->  104.8s] A deadlock is when two transactions each hold a lock that the other one needs,\n[ 104.8s ->  106.7s] so neither can continue.\n[ 106.7s ->  109.6s] I reproduced one in PostgreSQL.\n[ 109.6s ->  111.8s] Session 1 updated lab 1.\n[ 111.8s ->  113.3s] Then lab 2.\n[ 113.3s ->  123.9s] Session 2 updated lab 2, then lab 1. After about 1 second, PostgreSQL detected the deadlock and cancelled one transaction.\n[ 123.9s ->  132.4s] To avoid it, I update rows in the same order, keep transactions short, and retry the transaction that was cancelled.\n[ 132.4s ->  142.6s] 401, unauthorized, means the server doesn't know who you are. The token is missing or expired, so the client has to log in again.\n[ 142.6s ->  149.3s] 403, forbidden, means the server knows who you are, but you're not allowed to do this.\n[ 149.3s ->  155.4s] For example, in our system a student who calls the approving point gets a 403,\n[ 155.4s ->  159.1s] because only lab managers can approve reservations.\n[ 159.1s ->  163.8s] The n plus 1 problem is when the code runs one query to load a list,\n[ 163.8s ->  167.2s] and then one more query for each item in the list.\n[ 167.2s ->  170.8s] With JPA it often happens with lazy relations.\n[ 170.8s ->  178.2s] We load 50 reservations, and then each reservation loads its lab, so that's 51 queries.\n[ 178.2s ->  184.2s] The fix is a join fetch, or an entity graph. I had a similar case in a code review.\n[ 184.2s ->  190.6s] My first fix ran one query per reservation, and my lead asked me to load the lab's ones.\n[ 190.6s ->  197.2s] I'd like to join FPT software, because I want to work on real projects for foreign clients,\n[ 197.2s ->  200.5s] and learn how a large team delivers software.\n[ 200.5s ->  203.4s] I've already practiced that way of working.\n[ 203.4s ->  207.9s] I write tickets, pull requests, and queue and lists in English,\n[ 207.9s ->  211.0s] and I record my stand-ups to check them.\n[ 211.0s ->  214.2s] During the internship, I want to become a reliable\n[ 214.2s ->  217.0s] Mac and Developer in Java, and spring,\n[ 217.0s ->  220.5s] and get better at working with Japanese clients.\n[ 220.5s ->  223.0s] Yes, I have two questions.\n[ 223.0s ->  227.0s] What does a typical first month look like for an intern on your team?\n[ 227.2s ->  234.2s] And how do you give feedback to interns, for example in code reviews or one-to-one meetings?\n--- 577 words in 234.2 s = 148 words per minute\n","thuat-ngu-pv-sua.tsv":"# term\taccept (regex, case-insensitive)\tIPA (US; Cambridge when the word is in it)\tVietnamese meaning\tphrase in the answers\nencapsulation\tencapsulation\t/ɪnˌkæp.səˈleɪ.ʃən/\tđóng gói\tThat's encapsulation\npolymorphism\tpolymorphism\t/ˌpɑː.liˈmɔːr.fɪ.zəm/\tđa hình\tPolymorphism: our code\ninheritance\tinheritance\t/ɪnˈher.ɪ.təns/\tkế thừa\tI use inheritance less\nabstraction\tabstraction\t/æbˈstræk.ʃən/\ttrừu tượng\tpolymorphism and abstraction\nHashMap\thash ?map\t/ˈhæʃ mæp/\tbảng băm (Java)\tA HashMap stores\nhashCode\thash ?code\t/ˈhæʃ koʊd/\tmã băm\tIt calls hashCode\nbucket\tbuckets?\t/ˈbʌk.ɪt/\tngăn (của bảng băm)\tinside that bucket\nB-tree\tb-?tree\t/ˈbiː triː/\tcây B\tusually a B-tree\nsequential scan\tsequential scan\t/sɪˈkwen.ʃəl skæn/\tquét tuần tự\tdid a sequential scan\ndeadlock\tdeadlock\t/ˈded.lɑːk/\tkhoá chết\tA deadlock is when\ntransaction\ttransactions?\t/trænˈzæk.ʃən/\tgiao dịch\tkeep transactions short\nUnauthorized\tunauthori[sz]ed\t/ʌnˈɑː.θɚ.aɪzd/\tchưa xác thực (401)\tFour oh one, Unauthorized\nForbidden\tforbidden\t/fɚˈbɪd.ən/\tbị cấm (403)\tFour oh three, Forbidden\nendpoint\tendpoint\t/ˈend.pɔɪnt/\tđiểm cuối API\tcalls the approve endpoint\nN+1\tn ?\\+ ?1|n plus (one|1)\t/en plʌs wʌn/\tlỗi N+1 truy vấn\tThe N plus one problem\nJPA\tjpa|j\\.p\\.a\\.\t/ˌdʒeɪ.piːˈeɪ/\tJava Persistence API\tWith JPA it often happens\nentity graph\tentity graph\t/ˈen.t̬ə.t̬i ɡræf/\tđồ thị thực thể (JPA)\tor an entity graph\ninternship\tinternship\t/ˈɪn.tɝːn.ʃɪp/\tkỳ thực tập\tDuring the internship\n","thuat-ngu-pv.tsv":"# term\taccept (regex, case-insensitive)\tIPA (US; Cambridge when the word is in it)\tVietnamese meaning\tphrase in the answers\nencapsulation\tencapsulation\t/ɪnˌkæp.səˈleɪ.ʃən/\tđóng gói\tThat's encapsulation\npolymorphism\tpolymorphism\t/ˌpɑː.liˈmɔːr.fɪ.zəm/\tđa hình\tPolymorphism: our code\ninheritance\tinheritance\t/ɪnˈher.ɪ.təns/\tkế thừa\tI use inheritance less\nabstraction\tabstraction\t/æbˈstræk.ʃən/\ttrừu tượng\tpolymorphism and abstraction\nHashMap\thash ?map\t/ˈhæʃ mæp/\tbảng băm (Java)\tA HashMap stores\nhashCode\thash ?code\t/ˈhæʃ koʊd/\tmã băm\tIt calls hashCode\nbucket\tbuckets?\t/ˈbʌk.ɪt/\tngăn (của bảng băm)\tinside that bucket\nB-tree\tb-?tree\t/ˈbiː triː/\tcây B\tusually a B-tree\nsequential scan\tsequential scan\t/sɪˈkwen.ʃəl skæn/\tquét tuần tự\tdid a sequential scan\ndeadlock\tdeadlock\t/ˈded.lɑːk/\tkhoá chết\tA deadlock is when\ntransaction\ttransactions?\t/trænˈzæk.ʃən/\tgiao dịch\tkeep transactions short\nUnauthorized\tunauthori[sz]ed\t/ʌnˈɑː.θɚ.aɪzd/\tchưa xác thực (401)\tFour oh one, Unauthorized\nForbidden\tforbidden\t/fɚˈbɪd.ən/\tbị cấm (403)\tFour oh three, Forbidden\nendpoint\tendpoint\t/ˈend.pɔɪnt/\tđiểm cuối API\tcalls the approve endpoint\nN+1\tn ?\\+ ?1|n plus one\t/en plʌs wʌn/\tlỗi N+1 truy vấn\tThe N plus one problem\nJPA\tjpa|j\\.p\\.a\\.\t/ˌdʒeɪ.piːˈeɪ/\tJava Persistence API\tWith JPA it often happens\nentity graph\tentity graph\t/ˈen.t̬ə.t̬i ɡræf/\tđồ thị thực thể (JPA)\tor an entity graph\ninternship\tinternship\t/ˈɪn.tɝːn.ʃɪp/\tkỳ thực tập\tDuring the internship\n","tra-loi-1.txt":"Object-oriented programming has four main ideas: encapsulation, inheritance, polymorphism and abstraction.\nIn my lab booking project, the Reservation class keeps its status private. You can only change it through methods like approve or release. That's encapsulation: no other class can set an invalid status.\nPolymorphism: our code calls send on a Notifier interface, and the email version or a test version runs.\nI use inheritance less, because a deep class tree is hard to change.\n","tra-loi-2.txt":"A HashMap stores key-value pairs in an array of buckets. It calls hashCode on the key to choose a bucket, and equals to find the right key inside that bucket.\nSo if a class used as a key doesn't override both methods, get returns null for a key that looks the same. I tested it: with a plain class the lookup returned null, and with a Java record it worked.\nLookups are constant time on average. And never change a key after you put it in the map.\n","tra-loi-3.txt":"An index is a separate sorted structure, usually a B-tree, so the database can find rows without reading the whole table.\nI tested it in PostgreSQL with two hundred thousand reservations. Without an index, the query did a sequential scan. With an index on the student code, it used an index scan.\nBut the index didn't help when I wrapped the column in the lower function. Indexes also make inserts slower, so I add them for real queries, not for every column.\n","tra-loi-4.txt":"A deadlock is when two transactions each hold a lock that the other one needs, so neither can continue.\nI reproduced one in PostgreSQL. Session one updated lab one, then lab two. Session two updated lab two, then lab one. After about one second, PostgreSQL detected the deadlock and cancelled one transaction.\nTo avoid it, I update rows in the same order, keep transactions short, and retry the transaction that was cancelled.\n","tra-loi-5.txt":"Four oh one, Unauthorized, means the server doesn't know who you are. The token is missing or expired, so the client has to log in again.\nFour oh three, Forbidden, means the server knows who you are, but you're not allowed to do this.\nFor example, in our system a student who calls the approve endpoint gets a four oh three, because only lab managers can approve reservations.\n","tra-loi-5b.txt":"Four oh one, Unauthorized, means the server doesn't know who you are. The token is missing or expired, so the client has to log in again.\nFour oh three, Forbidden, means the server knows who you are, but you're not allowed to do this.\nFor example, in our system a student who calls the endpoint for approvals gets a four oh three, because only lab managers can approve.\n","tra-loi-6.txt":"The N plus one problem is when the code runs one query to load a list, and then one more query for each item in the list.\nWith JPA it often happens with lazy relations. We load fifty reservations, and then each reservation loads its lab, so that's fifty-one queries.\nThe fix is a join fetch or an entity graph. I had a similar case in a code review: my first fix ran one query per reservation, and my lead asked me to load the labs once.\n","tra-loi-7.txt":"I'd like to join FPT Software because I want to work on real projects for foreign clients, and learn how a large team delivers software.\nI've already practised that way of working. I write tickets, pull requests and Q and A lists in English, and I record my stand-ups to check them.\nDuring the internship, I want to become a reliable backend developer in Java and Spring, and get better at working with Japanese clients.\n","tra-loi-8.txt":"Yes, I have two questions.\nWhat does a typical first month look like for an intern on your team?\nAnd how do you give feedback to interns, for example in code reviews or one-to-one meetings?\n"};
